    "text": "- **High-level description**: The function `merge_intervals` takes a list of intervals (each interval represented as a tuple of two integers) and merges overlapping intervals. It returns a new list of non-overlapping intervals sorted by their start times.\n\n- **Time complexity**: O(n log n), where n is the number of intervals. This is due to the sorting step, which dominates the time complexity.\n\n- **Space complexity**: O(n), as the function creates a new list `result` to store the merged intervals and uses additional space for sorting.\n\n- **Correctness bug**: The function does not handle the case where intervals are already sorted or when there is only one interval. While this is not a bug per se, it could be optimized by checking if the input list is already sorted before performing the sort operation.\n\n- **Performance improvement**: Instead of converting each interval to a list and back to a tuple, you can directly append tuples to the result list. This avoids unnecessary conversions and improves performance slightly. Specifically, change `current = list(sorted_intervals[0])` to `current = sorted_intervals[0]` and modify the line where `current` is updated to `current = (start, max(current[1], end))`.",
